    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d215">

                <pb facs="0554" n="520" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v07p1/pg520"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">762A.5/8–1753</note>
                <head>No. 215<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of
                            State to the Secretary of Defense (Wilson)</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d215-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-08-17">August 17, 1953</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: You will recall, in
                    approving NSC 160<anchor n="2" xml:id="d215-f2"
                    /> regarding Germany, the Council decided to insert at the end of paragraph 11b
                    the provision that “if developments should so indicate, it may be desirable to
                    take bilaterally with the West German government certain initial steps in the
                    actual creation of arming German units, if this can be done without serious
                    repercussions on our relations with France”.</p>
                <p>My understanding is that no action of any kind will be taken by your Department
                    pursuant to this provision unless the Department of State concurs that this can
                    be safely done without injuring our relations with France.</p>
                <pb facs="0555" n="521" xml:id="pg521"/>
                <p>I must stress my conviction that under present conditions any discussions with
                    the Germans along these lines could have very serious adverse effects in France
                    and in Germany. Furthermore, we must carefully guard against any chance of a
                    “leak” or unauthorized action by representatives of the State and Defense
                    Departments abroad.</p>
                <p>The review of the German policy at the first meeting in October, to which we also
                    agreed, will provide an occasion for further discussion of this question.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Foster
                                Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
